# config/caddy.env -- written by install.sh, read by Caddy alone as an
# env_file (spec, "How the services run"). Root-only at mode 0600: this is the
# one config file that holds a credential able to change DNS.
#
# THAT CREDENTIAL COMES FROM --dns-env-file, NEVER FROM --dns-env. The DNS
# provider's secret can rewrite the zone for this domain, which means it can
# point the apex and every tenant host anywhere and mint a certificate for
# them. A value passed as `--dns-env AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=...` stays in root's
# shell history and in `ps` output for the whole install, and install.sh can
# clean up neither -- the same reasoning that made --platform-key-file a path.
# Write this file, chmod 600 it, pass it to --dns-env-file, and DELETE IT once
# install.sh has run. --dns-env remains for a NON-SECRET such as AWS_REGION.
#
# One NAME=VALUE per line. Blank lines and lines starting with # are skipped;
# anything else must be a name (a letter or underscore, then letters, digits
# and underscores) and a value of printable, space-free characters. A line with
# no `=` is refused here, because Compose accepts it and leaves the variable
# UNSET -- which looks exactly like a credential that was set.
#
# What goes in it depends on the caddy-dns module named by --dns-provider.
# Route 53 reads the standard AWS variables:
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=AKIAEXAMPLE
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=example
AWS_REGION=us-east-1
#
# Cloudflare instead takes its token as an argument to the dns directive, so
# the Caddyfile reads it through WAKU_DNS_PROVIDER:
#   --dns-provider 'cloudflare {env.CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN}'
#   with CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=... as a line in the --dns-env-file
#
# install.sh gives the whole string to the Caddyfile and the FIRST WORD of it
# to xcaddy, which is what makes that recipe build: the module is `cloudflare`
# and `{env.CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN}` is the directive's argument. An earlier
# version handed the whole string to both, so this documented recipe could not
# build at all -- xcaddy was asked for a module named
# "cloudflare {env.CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN}".
